# Extra sample courses. Added automatically (once) on start-up. Edit/delete them from the Admin panel.
def C(title, cat, lvl, dur, summ, desc, ins, bio, outs, lessons, quiz):
    return {'title': title, 'category': cat, 'level': lvl, 'duration': dur, 'summary': summ, 'description': desc,
            'instructor': ins, 'bio': bio, 'outcomes': outs, 'lessons': lessons, 'quiz': quiz}

MORE = [
C('JavaScript Essentials', 'Web Development', 'Intermediate', '5 hours',
  'Go beyond the basics and write modern, practical JavaScript.',
  'Learn the core of modern JavaScript: data types, functions, arrays, objects, the DOM and fetching data from APIs.',
  'Ananya Rao', 'Front-end developer and mentor who has helped hundreds of beginners ship their first website.',
  ['Use let, const and arrow functions', 'Transform data with array methods', 'Work with objects and JSON', 'Fetch data with async/await'],
  [('Variables, types and operators', 10, "## The building blocks\nUse `const` by default and `let` when a value changes. Main types: string, number, boolean, null, undefined, object.\n\n```\nconst price = 120;\nlet qty = 2;\nconsole.log(price * qty); // 240\n```\n\nPrefer `===` over `==` because it never converts types behind your back."),
   ('Functions and scope', 12, "## Reusable logic\n```\nfunction add(a, b) { return a + b; }\nconst mul = (a, b) => a * b;\n```\n\nVariables declared inside a function are **local** to it. A **closure** is a function that remembers variables from where it was created, which is how counters and private data are built."),
   ('Arrays and array methods', 14, "## Working with lists\n```\nconst nums = [1, 2, 3, 4];\nnums.map(n => n * 2);        // [2,4,6,8]\nnums.filter(n => n % 2 === 0); // [2,4]\nnums.reduce((s, n) => s + n, 0); // 10\n```\n\n`map` transforms, `filter` selects and `reduce` combines. None of them change the original array."),
   ('Objects and JSON', 12, "## Structured data\n```\nconst user = { name: 'Asha', skills: ['JS', 'CSS'] };\nconst { name } = user;      // destructuring\nconst copy = { ...user, age: 21 }; // spread\nconst text = JSON.stringify(user);\n```\n\n**JSON** is the standard text format for sending data between a browser and a server. Use `JSON.parse` to turn it back into an object."),
   ('The DOM and events', 14, "## Changing the page\n```\nconst list = document.querySelector('#todo');\nconst li = document.createElement('li');\nli.textContent = 'Learn JS';\nlist.append(li);\n```\n\nUse `addEventListener` for clicks and input. **Event delegation** means one listener on a parent handles all its children."),
   ('Async JavaScript and fetch', 14, "## Waiting for data\n```\nasync function load() {\n  try {\n    const res = await fetch('/api/items');\n    const data = await res.json();\n    console.log(data);\n  } catch (err) { console.error(err); }\n}\n```\n\nA **Promise** represents a future value. `async/await` lets you write asynchronous code that reads top to bottom. Always handle errors with `try/catch`.")],
  [('Which keyword declares a variable that cannot be reassigned?', ['let', 'var', 'const', 'set'], 2),
   ('Which array method creates a new array by transforming each item?', ['filter', 'map', 'push', 'splice'], 1),
   ('What does JSON.stringify do?', ['Parses text into an object', 'Turns an object into JSON text', 'Deletes an object', 'Sends a request'], 1),
   ('Which operator compares value AND type?', ['=', '==', '===', '=>'], 2),
   ('What does await do inside an async function?', ['Stops the whole browser', 'Pauses until a Promise settles', 'Creates a loop', 'Deletes the Promise'], 1),
   ('Event delegation means:', ['One listener on a parent handles child events', 'Deleting events', 'Using many timers', 'Blocking clicks'], 0)]),

C('SQL and Databases', 'Data & Office', 'Beginner', '4 hours',
  'Ask questions of data using the language every database understands.',
  'Learn how relational databases store data and how to read, filter, join, summarise and change it with SQL.',
  'Karthik Menon', 'Data analyst who trains teams in spreadsheet and reporting skills.',
  ['Explain tables, rows and keys', 'Query data with SELECT, WHERE and ORDER BY', 'Combine tables with JOIN', 'Summarise data with GROUP BY'],
  [('What is a relational database?', 8, "## Tables that relate\nData is stored in **tables** made of rows (records) and columns (fields). A **primary key** uniquely identifies a row. A **foreign key** points to a row in another table, such as `orders.customer_id` linking to `customers.id`.\n\nPopular systems: MySQL, PostgreSQL, SQLite and SQL Server."),
   ('SELECT, WHERE and ORDER BY', 12, "## Reading data\n```\nSELECT name, city\nFROM customers\nWHERE city = 'Chennai' AND age >= 18\nORDER BY name ASC\nLIMIT 10;\n```\n\nUse `LIKE 'A%'` for pattern matching, `IN (...)` for lists and `BETWEEN` for ranges."),
   ('Aggregates and GROUP BY', 12, "## Summarising\n```\nSELECT city, COUNT(*) AS total, AVG(age) AS avg_age\nFROM customers\nGROUP BY city\nHAVING COUNT(*) > 5;\n```\n\n`COUNT`, `SUM`, `AVG`, `MIN` and `MAX` work on groups. Use `WHERE` to filter rows before grouping and `HAVING` to filter groups after."),
   ('Joining tables', 14, "## Combining data\n```\nSELECT c.name, o.total\nFROM customers c\nINNER JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.id;\n```\n\n- **INNER JOIN** keeps only matching rows.\n- **LEFT JOIN** keeps every row from the left table, even without a match.\n\nAlways join on keys to avoid accidental huge results."),
   ('INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE', 10, "## Changing data\n```\nINSERT INTO customers(name, city) VALUES ('Asha', 'Chennai');\nUPDATE customers SET city = 'Madurai' WHERE id = 7;\nDELETE FROM customers WHERE id = 7;\n```\n\n**Always include WHERE** on UPDATE and DELETE, otherwise every row changes. Try a SELECT with the same WHERE first.")],
  [('What uniquely identifies a row in a table?', ['Foreign key', 'Primary key', 'Index name', 'Column alias'], 1),
   ('Which clause filters rows before grouping?', ['HAVING', 'WHERE', 'LIMIT', 'ORDER BY'], 1),
   ('Which JOIN keeps all rows from the left table?', ['INNER JOIN', 'LEFT JOIN', 'CROSS ONLY', 'SELF STOP'], 1),
   ('Which function counts rows?', ['SUM()', 'COUNT()', 'TOTAL()', 'ROWS()'], 1),
   ('What happens if you run DELETE without WHERE?', ['Nothing', 'Only one row is deleted', 'All rows are deleted', 'An index is added'], 2),
   ('Which keyword sorts results?', ['SORTBY', 'ORDER BY', 'GROUP', 'ARRANGE'], 1)]),

C('Cyber Security Awareness', 'IT & Security', 'Beginner', '3 hours',
  'Protect yourself and your organisation from the most common online threats.',
  'Understand how attackers think and learn the everyday habits that keep accounts, devices and data safe.',
  'Daniel Moore', 'Software engineer who loves teaching technology through small, practical projects.',
  ['Spot phishing and social engineering', 'Create and manage strong passwords', 'Secure devices and networks', 'Know what to do after an incident'],
  [('Why security matters', 8, "## The CIA triad\nSecurity protects three things: **Confidentiality** (only the right people see data), **Integrity** (data is not altered) and **Availability** (systems work when needed).\n\nMost attacks target people, not technology, because tricking a person is often easier than breaking a system."),
   ('Passwords and multi-factor authentication', 10, "## Strong accounts\n- Use long passphrases (14+ characters) that are unique per site.\n- Store them in a **password manager**.\n- Turn on **multi-factor authentication (MFA)** everywhere; an app or security key is stronger than SMS.\n\nNever share one-time codes with anyone, even if they claim to be support."),
   ('Phishing and social engineering', 12, "## Think before you click\nSigns of phishing: urgent threats, unknown senders, mismatched links, unexpected attachments and requests for passwords or money.\n\nHover over links to preview the address, open sites by typing them yourself, and verify unusual requests through another channel such as a phone call."),
   ('Malware and safe devices', 10, "## Keeping devices clean\n**Malware** includes viruses, ransomware and spyware. Reduce risk by installing updates quickly, using only official app stores, running antivirus, locking screens and keeping **backups** offline or in the cloud. Backups are the best defence against ransomware."),
   ('Safe networks and responding to incidents', 10, "## On Wi-Fi and after a problem\nAvoid sensitive logins on public Wi-Fi or use a trusted **VPN**. Prefer websites that start with `https://`.\n\nIf you suspect a breach: disconnect the device, change passwords from a clean device, enable MFA, report to your IT team or the bank and keep evidence such as screenshots.")],
  [('Which is part of the CIA triad?', ['Confidentiality', 'Cookies', 'Caching', 'Compression'], 0),
   ('What is the best protection against ransomware?', ['Reusing passwords', 'Regular backups', 'Disabling updates', 'Public Wi-Fi'], 1),
   ('A message demands you act immediately and click a link. This is a sign of:', ['Phishing', 'A software update', 'A backup', 'MFA'], 0),
   ('What does MFA add?', ['Faster internet', 'An extra proof of identity', 'More storage', 'Free software'], 1),
   ('A good password is:', ['Short and memorable', 'The same everywhere', 'Long and unique', 'Your birthday'], 2),
   ('What should you do first if a device is breached?', ['Keep using it', 'Disconnect it and change passwords from a clean device', 'Post about it online', 'Delete all backups'], 1)]),

C('Project Management Fundamentals', 'Business', 'Beginner', '4 hours',
  'Plan, run and deliver projects on time using proven methods.',
  'Learn the language of project management: scope, schedule, risks, stakeholders and agile ways of working.',
  'Priya Nair', 'Growth marketer who has managed campaigns and cross-team launches for start-ups.',
  ['Define scope, goals and deliverables', 'Build a simple schedule and budget', 'Manage risks and stakeholders', 'Run an agile sprint'],
  [('What is a project?', 8, "## Temporary and unique\nA project is a **temporary** effort with a clear start and end that creates a **unique** result. Success is balanced across **scope, time and cost**, with quality at the centre. Change one and the others are affected."),
   ('Defining scope and goals', 12, "## Start with the why\nWrite SMART goals: **S**pecific, **M**easurable, **A**chievable, **R**elevant, **T**ime-bound.\n\nBreak the work into a **work breakdown structure (WBS)**: deliverables, then tasks. Write down what is **out of scope** to prevent scope creep."),
   ('Scheduling and budgeting', 12, "## Time and money\nList tasks, estimate durations, mark **dependencies** and show them in a **Gantt chart**. The **critical path** is the longest chain of dependent tasks; if it slips, the whole project slips.\n\nBuild a budget from labour, tools and a 10-15% contingency."),
   ('Risks and stakeholders', 10, "## Expect surprises\nA **risk register** lists each risk with probability, impact and a response plan (avoid, reduce, transfer or accept).\n\nA **stakeholder** is anyone affected by the project. Map them by influence and interest, and communicate with each on a regular, agreed schedule."),
   ('Agile and Scrum basics', 12, "## Deliver in small steps\nAgile delivers value in short cycles called **sprints** (1-4 weeks). In Scrum the team keeps a **product backlog**, plans a sprint, meets daily for a 15-minute **stand-up**, shows work in a **review** and improves in a **retrospective**. Roles: Product Owner, Scrum Master and the Development Team.")],
  [('A project is best described as:', ['Ongoing forever', 'Temporary and creating a unique result', 'A daily routine', 'Only for software'], 1),
   ('What does the T in SMART stand for?', ['Technical', 'Time-bound', 'Total', 'Trusted'], 1),
   ('The critical path is:', ['The cheapest tasks', 'The longest chain of dependent tasks', 'The first task only', 'A risk list'], 1),
   ('Which document tracks risks and responses?', ['Gantt chart', 'Risk register', 'Backlog', 'Invoice'], 1),
   ('How long is a typical Scrum sprint?', ['1-4 weeks', '1 year', '1 hour', '6 months'], 0),
   ('Scope creep means:', ['Uncontrolled growth of project work', 'Finishing early', 'Cutting the budget', 'Hiring staff'], 0)]),

C('Communication and Soft Skills', 'Career', 'Beginner', '3 hours',
  'Speak, write and work with people confidently in any workplace.',
  'Build the soft skills employers look for: clear communication, listening, teamwork, time management and interview confidence.',
  'Meera Iyer', 'Trainer and mentor who coaches students and graduates for their first jobs.',
  ['Communicate clearly in speech and writing', 'Listen actively', 'Manage your time and priorities', 'Prepare for interviews'],
  [('The communication process', 8, "## Sender, message, receiver\nCommunication works when the receiver understands what the sender meant. Barriers include noise, assumptions, jargon and emotions. Use the **7 Cs**: clear, concise, concrete, correct, coherent, complete and courteous."),
   ('Active listening', 10, "## Listening to understand\n- Give full attention and put the phone away.\n- Do not interrupt.\n- **Paraphrase**: \"So you mean...\"\n- Ask open questions such as \"What happened next?\"\n- Notice body language and tone.\n\nPeople trust those who make them feel heard."),
   ('Professional writing and email', 10, "## Write so it gets read\nUse a specific subject line, greet politely, state your purpose in the first sentence, keep paragraphs short and end with a clear next step.\n\nProofread for tone and spelling before pressing send, and use Reply All only when everyone needs the answer."),
   ('Teamwork and time management', 12, "## Getting more done together\nAgree on roles, share updates early and handle conflict by discussing the issue, not the person.\n\nFor time, use the **Eisenhower matrix**: urgent and important (do now), important not urgent (schedule), urgent not important (delegate) and neither (drop)."),
   ('Interview skills', 12, "## Show your value\nResearch the company, practise answers aloud and use the **STAR** method: **S**ituation, **T**ask, **A**ction, **R**esult. Dress neatly, arrive early, make eye contact and prepare two questions to ask the interviewer.")],
  [('Which method structures an interview answer?', ['STAR', 'SMART', 'SWOT', 'SEO'], 0),
   ('Paraphrasing helps you:', ['Avoid listening', 'Confirm you understood', 'Speak louder', 'End the talk'], 1),
   ('Which is one of the 7 Cs?', ['Concise', 'Complicated', 'Casual', 'Cryptic'], 0),
   ('In the Eisenhower matrix, important but not urgent tasks should be:', ['Dropped', 'Scheduled', 'Delegated', 'Ignored'], 1),
   ('A good email subject line is:', ['Empty', 'Specific', 'All capital letters', 'Very long'], 1)]),

C('Data Science with Python', 'Programming', 'Intermediate', '5 hours',
  'Load, clean, explore and visualise data using pandas and charts.',
  'A hands-on introduction to the data science workflow in Python, from raw CSV files to insights and simple predictions.',
  'Karthik Menon', 'Data analyst who trains teams in Python, spreadsheets and reporting.',
  ['Load and inspect data with pandas', 'Clean missing and messy values', 'Summarise and visualise data', 'Explain a simple machine-learning model'],
  [('The data science workflow', 8, "## From question to insight\n1. Ask a clear question.\n2. Collect data.\n3. Clean it.\n4. Explore and visualise.\n5. Model.\n6. Communicate results.\n\nMost real projects spend more than half their time on cleaning, so learn it well."),
   ('Getting started with pandas', 14, "## DataFrames\n```\nimport pandas as pd\ndf = pd.read_csv('sales.csv')\nprint(df.head())\nprint(df.shape)\ndf.info()\n```\n\nA **DataFrame** is a table. Select a column with `df['price']` and rows with `df[df['price'] > 100]`."),
   ('Cleaning data', 14, "## Make it trustworthy\n```\ndf.isna().sum()                  # count missing\ndf['age'] = df['age'].fillna(df['age'].median())\ndf = df.drop_duplicates()\ndf['date'] = pd.to_datetime(df['date'])\n```\n\nCheck data types, fix typos in categories and remove impossible values like negative ages."),
   ('Grouping and visualising', 14, "## Find patterns\n```\nby_region = df.groupby('region')['sales'].sum()\nby_region.plot(kind='bar')\n```\n\nUse `describe()` for quick statistics. Pick **histograms** for distributions, **line charts** for time and **scatter plots** for relationships between two numbers."),
   ('A first machine-learning model', 14, "## Learning from examples\nIn **supervised learning** a model learns from labelled data. Split data into **training** and **test** sets so you can measure accuracy on data the model has not seen.\n\n```\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\nX_tr, X_te, y_tr, y_te = train_test_split(X, y, test_size=0.2)\nmodel = LinearRegression().fit(X_tr, y_tr)\nprint(model.score(X_te, y_te))\n```\n\nA model that is perfect on training data but poor on test data is **overfitting**.")],
  [('Which library provides the DataFrame?', ['pandas', 'turtle', 'flask', 'random'], 0),
   ('Which command counts missing values per column?', ['df.isna().sum()', 'df.head()', 'df.plot()', 'df.sort()'], 0),
   ('Which chart best shows the relationship between two numbers?', ['Pie', 'Scatter plot', 'Table', 'Bar of names'], 1),
   ('Why use a test set?', ['To make training faster', 'To measure performance on unseen data', 'To delete data', 'To rename columns'], 1),
   ('Overfitting means:', ['Great on training data but poor on new data', 'Too little data', 'A missing column', 'A slow computer'], 0),
   ('Which step usually takes the most time in a project?', ['Cleaning data', 'Choosing a colour', 'Saving the file', 'Printing'], 0)]),
]
